-- title:
--   Quotient map from the chart presentation of Mumford's scheme
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $r$ is a prime, $\mathcal O$ is a discrete valuation domain (the hypothesis `hdvr` asserting the discrete valuation ring structure is passed explicitly), $\pi \in \mathcal O$ is irreducible (`hπ`), the residue ring $\mathcal O/(\pi)$ has exactly $r$ elements (`hres`), and $K_0$ is a field which is an $\mathcal O$-algebra and a fraction field of $\mathcal O$. Further, $g_1 \in \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ is required by `hg₁` to be the diagonal matrix $\mathrm{diag}(\operatorname{algebraMap} \pi, 1)$, and $N$ is a subgroup of $\mathrm{PGL}(2,K_0)$.
--
--   The functor $\Omega =$ `Omega K₀ π` sends an $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ to the type of Deligne data `DeligneDatum π B`: a choice, for every full lattice $M \subseteq K_0^2$ over $\mathcal O$, of a $B$-submodule $\mathrm{line}(M)$ of the base change $B \otimes_{\mathcal O} M$, such that each quotient $(B\otimes_{\mathcal O} M)/\mathrm{line}(M)$ is an invertible $B$-module, the lines are monotone for inclusions of lattices, are equivariant for the homothety action of $K_0^\times$, and satisfy the nondegeneracy condition at every prime $\mathfrak p$ of $B$ recorded in the structure `DeligneDatum`; on morphisms $\Omega$ acts by base change of the lines. For $g \in \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$, `DeligneDatum.IsPullback B g d d'` says that $\mathrm{line}_{d'}(M)$ is the preimage of $\mathrm{line}_d(g\cdot M)$ under the base-changed lattice isomorphism, for every $M$.
--
--   Finally, `Gl : MumfordGlue 𝒪 π K₀ r g₁ N` is a Mumford glue datum: a family of schemes `Gl.Z n`, base morphisms `Gl.zb n : Gl.Z n ⟶ Spec (𝒪/(π^(n+1)))` which are flat and separated, transition morphisms `Gl.zt n : Gl.Z n ⟶ Gl.Z (n+1)` exhibiting `Gl.Z n` as the fibre product of `Gl.Z (n+1)` with $\operatorname{Spec} \mathcal O/(\pi^{n+1})$, and chart morphisms `Gl.ζ h n` from the spectrum of `chartERing 𝒪 π r` modulo $\pi^{n+1}$, indexed by $h \in \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$, which are open immersions over the base, compatible with the transitions, cover each `Gl.Z n` by finitely many translates, are unchanged under left multiplication of $h$ by elements of $N$, and satisfy the overlap relation `ζ_rel` of that structure (all these fields are summarised here).
--
--   Under these hypotheses there exists a family
--   $$q_n : \Omega(B) \longrightarrow \operatorname{Hom}(\operatorname{Spec} B, \ \mathtt{Gl.Z}\ n),$$
--   given for every $n \in \mathbb N$, every $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ with $(\operatorname{algebraMap}_{\mathcal O \to B} \pi)^{n+1} = 0$ (this nilpotency hypothesis being an argument of $q$), and every Deligne datum $P$ over $B$, with the following nine properties.
--
--   First, $q_n(P)$ lies over the base: $q_n(P)$ followed by `Gl.zb n` followed by $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the quotient map $\mathcal O \to \mathcal O/(\pi^{n+1})$ equals $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the structure morphism $\mathcal O \to B$.
--
--   Second, naturality: for $\mathcal O$-algebras $B, B'$ both killed by $\pi^{n+1}$ in the above sense, every $\mathcal O$-algebra map $\varphi : B \to B'$ and every $P \in \Omega(B)$, one has $q_n(\Omega(\varphi)P) = \operatorname{Spec}(\varphi)$ followed by $q_n(P)$.
--
--   Third, compatibility with the tower: if $B$ is killed by both $\pi^{n+1}$ and $\pi^{n+2}$ in the above sense, then $q_{n+1}(P) = q_n(P)$ followed by `Gl.zt n`.
--
--   Fourth, $N$-invariance: for $g \in \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ whose class in $\mathrm{PGL}(2,K_0)$ lies in $N$, and Deligne data $P, P'$ over $B$ with $P'$ the pullback of $P$ along $g^{-1}$, one has $q_n(P') = q_n(P)$.
--
--   Fifth, the charts are open immersions. Write $A_n$ for the quotient of `chartERing 𝒪 π r` by the ideal generated by the $(n+1)$-st power of the image of $\pi$, and let `hA` be the corresponding nilpotency hypothesis. If $d$ is a Deligne datum over $A_n$ which is tautological in the sense that its line at the standard full lattice is the $A_n$-span of $\overline{\xi} \otimes e_0 + 1 \otimes e_1$, its line at $g_1 \cdot (\text{standard lattice})$ is the image under the base-changed lattice isomorphism of the span of $1 \otimes e_0 + \overline{\eta} \otimes e_1$ (with $\xi =$ `chartERing.ξ 𝒪 π r`, $\eta =$ `chartERing.η 𝒪 π r` and $e_0, e_1$ the standard basis vectors of the standard lattice), and $d$ satisfies `DeligneDatum.InEdgeChart π (g₁ · stdFullLattice) stdFullLattice`, i.e. the edge nondegeneracy condition at every prime of $A_n$; and if $P$ is the pullback of $d$ along $h^{-1}$ for some $h \in \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$, then $q_n(P)$ is an open immersion.
--
--   Sixth, these charts cover: for every $n$ and every point $z$ of `Gl.Z n` there exist $h \in \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ and Deligne data $d, P$ over $A_n$ with $d$ tautological and in the edge chart as in the previous clause, $P$ the pullback of $d$ along $h^{-1}$, and $z$ in the range of the underlying map of topological spaces of $q_n(P)$.
--
--   Seventh, fibres over local rings are exactly the $N$-orbits: if $B$ is in addition a local ring, then for $P, P' \in \Omega(B)$ one has $q_n(P) = q_n(P')$ if and only if there exists $g \in \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ with class in $N$ such that $P'$ is the pullback of $P$ along $g^{-1}$.
--
--   Eighth, a Zariski-local version over an arbitrary $B$: if $q_n(P) = q_n(P')$, then there are a finite type $\iota$ and a family $f : \iota \to B$ whose range generates the unit ideal such that for every $i$ and every $\mathcal O$-algebra and $B$-algebra $C$ (compatibly, as a scalar tower) which is a localisation of $B$ away from $f(i)$, there exists $g \in \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ with class in $N$ such that the image of $P'$ in $\Omega(C)$ is the pullback along $g^{-1}$ of the image of $P$.
--
--   Ninth, the levelwise universal property: for every $n$, every scheme $T$ and every family $\rho$ assigning to each $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ killed by $\pi^{n+1}$ as above and each $P \in \Omega(B)$ a morphism $\operatorname{Spec} B \to T$, which is natural in $B$ in the same sense as the second clause and invariant under pullback by elements of $\mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ with class in $N$ as in the fourth clause, there is a unique morphism $u : \mathtt{Gl.Z}\ n \to T$ such that $q_n(P)$ followed by $u$ equals $\rho(P)$ for all such $B$ and all $P$.
--
--   This is the functor-of-points description of the quotient morphism from the chart presentation of Mumford's formal scheme at level $n$: it turns a Mumford glue datum into a representation of the $N$-quotient of the Deligne-datum functor $\hat\Omega$ modulo $\pi^{n+1}$, with charts, orbit fibres and a levelwise universal property. It is used in the construction of the Mumford tower for a Schottky subgroup, via [`CerednikDrinfeld.FormalOmega.nonempty_mumfordTower_of_isSchottky`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.FormalOmega.nonempty_mumfordTower_of_isSchottky), in the Čerednik–Drinfel'd uniformisation part of the argument.
